  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Chance for showers/thunderstorms in Lemhi County and southwest\n   Montana on Tuesday\n\n-  Warm, dry week ahead for the rest of the region.\n\n-  Showers return late next week or early next weekend.\n\n\nSatellite imagery early this morning shows dry air under a ridge\nof high pressure over the northwestern U.S. including the \nNorthern Rockies. A closed circulation of low pressure is spinning\nover central California and moving inland. The ridge of high \npressure will keep the Northern Rockies dry today, with high \ntemperatures about 10 degrees above normal. Patchy morning fog \nwill affect some valleys of western Montana. Fog will remain in \nthe forecast into midweek. The California low will track northeast\ntoday and on Tuesday. By Tuesday afternoon, moisture associated \nwith that system will enter Lemhi County and southwest Montana, \nincluding Salmon and Butte, resulting in chances for showers and \npossibly thunderstorms in the late afternoon and evening. The \nmoisture feed is short-lived, though, and dry conditions with \nabove-normal temperatures will continue through Thursday. \n\nA shift towards more seasonable temperatures and chances for\nprecipitation arrive on Friday. Most model ensemble members (about\n75%) are in favor of a trough of low pressure digging along the \nCanadian coast and moving into the Pacific Northwest by Saturday.\nDetails on this event are still unclear, as there is little \nagreement on timing and intensity of this system. Regardless of\nprecipitation chances, expect a shift back towards seasonable\ntemperatures on Friday lasting through next weekend.",
